Z||000000||wxxo||05-19-2000||01:52 PM||||help me out here. where is the solonoid? is it the thing with the diaphragm in it. or is it on the other side of the engine with the big hose in the back and the two smaller hoses coming out of the frount. (i got a v6 btw) i took that piece off and i can blow air through it. tasts like *&!@ and i dont recomend doing it yourself. but anyhow ive had that code 32 problem since i got the thing. the egr valve (diaphragm thing) is brand spankin new but that didnt solve the problem. what could it be??||||reg||1
Z||000001||ka4nkf||05-19-2000||02:18 PM||||Code 32 will be your solenoid. the one with the larger hose in the back and two smaller hoses in front, also electrical connector.location is by thermostat housing.this solenoid runs about $100.00.they go bad very often.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||05-19-2000||02:46 PM||||Another thing that can cause a code 32 is a bad vaccum line either between the source and the solenoid or between the solenoid and the EGR. Check that out first since it will be much cheaper. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gokart||05-19-2000||10:08 PM||||I have a brand new solenoid in from the Fiero Store and all vaccum lines are attached. STILL getting 32 when I get about 75/80 mph. Wondering now if it could be a leak at the end of the tube where it meets the plenum because I have the wrong gasket in place?http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/003771.html||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||05-20-2000||01:56 AM||||The EGR vacuum switch, which is responsible for setting the code 32, is not effected by the EGR tube whatsoever.  If you are getting codes set with a new EGR solenoid, your problem is most likely a clogged or leaking EGR solenoid vacuum supply line.  Carbon build-up in the throttle body area can easily clog the vacuum port where the supply line attaches. If no problems with the EGR vacuum supply exist, then I would suspect either an EGR harness electrical fault or a defective ECM.<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gokart||05-20-2000||09:43 PM||||I have the 87 shop manual and it has the codes listed in numerical order. Code 32 also says if the egr VALVE does not move when connected to a vacuum pump to replace valve. So it could be more than just the solenoid and the vacuum supply going to it that sets code 32 if the valve is not working. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||mwbackus||05-21-2000||12:52 AM||||Where did you get the impression that I somehow stated that "EGR valve" operation does not effect the setting of the code?  I stated that the EGR tube will not set a code 32. Period!<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gokart||05-21-2000||06:31 AM||||That info wasn't directed towards you Mike. Not out to prove anyone wrong. Just something I wanted to post letting others know that are having the egr woes that I'm having that it can be something other than the "switch" or the "solenoid" that can set a code 32. PEACE! Marc.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fieroshop||05-21-2000||10:27 AM||||Well lets see said the old man. What is the problem? Code 32 is set byt the solenoid and only that. If you have a leak in the vacume line going to the egr solenoid then it will set th same code. Check your vacume line going to the solenoid and if that is okay replace your solenoid. Someone mentioned that they replace theirs with a new one, well I've seen new ones being defective.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Gokart||05-21-2000||09:14 PM||||No offense, but I'm gonna go with my factory service manual on this one. It DOES state in black and white code 32 can be set by a faulty valve. It'll be alot cheaper replacing the $30.00 valve as my next move to solve this prob than spending another $90.00 on another solenoid or telling the Fiero Store they sold me a faulty product. I'm on vaca starting the 27th and this code will be gone for good by the time I go back to work.||||reg||1
